// InsertRangeChecks — the P1.9 ranged-typealias guard pass. A Std→Std pass that turns the parser's
// recorded range-check sites (`project.rangeChecks`) into the compile-time E3005s and runtime panics a
// ranged typealias promises at EVERY position a value meets one: a narrowing `as` cast, a ranged
// `return`, a struct-literal field, a field store, a field's declared default, an array element and a
// call argument. It runs right after `lowerMaxonToStd`, before the other Std passes.
//
// ⚠ **WHICH HALVES A POSITION OWES IS THE SITE'S TO SAY, NOT THIS PASS'S** — see `RangeCheckGuard`. The
// compile-time E3005 is owed EVERYWHERE and is raised by the one decision function
// (`needsRuntimeGuard`); the runtime cascade is skipped at the two positions where the value has already
// travelled into a call by the time a guard could land (a call argument, and an array element, which is
// one).
//
// ⭐ WHY STD-TIER, AND NOT THE MAXON-TIER MARKER OP THE PLAN SKETCHED. The narrowing guard needs a
// primitive the Maxon dialect does not have — "abort with a symbolized backtrace and exit 1" — and the
// only way to add one at the Maxon tier is a new `MaxonOp` variant, which forces a fresh arm into EVERY
// exhaustive `MaxonOp` match, including the escape-analysis functions in `SemanticCheck.maxon` /
// `FnRefThunk.maxon` that the closures/async rung owns and is actively editing. So the guard is emitted
// HERE instead, where the panic is a PLAIN `StdOp.call` to the `mrt_panic` runtime chunk (no new op in
// any dialect) and the compare cascade is the ordinary `cmp`/`condBranch` the tier already carries. The
// cast alias name and source spans — which the Std tier's ops do not carry — ride the parser's
// `project.rangeChecks` side table instead of an op.
//
// ⭐ ONE GUARD EMITTER, one unsigned strategy, one panic-message builder, serving BOTH the cast sites and
// the return sites (`emitGuardAt`). This is the whole point of the rewrite: v1 emits the guard TWICE (a
// Maxon `expandCastRangeChecks` for casts, a Std `insertRangeChecks` for returns) and the two copies have
// drifted on unsigned handling. Here there is one.
//
// ⚠ SIGNED COMPARES, in a CASCADE. shv2's Std tier has no unsigned integer compare (`StdCmpPred` is
// signed only) and no `setcc`, so a two-bound check is a CASCADE of fused `cmp`+`condBranch` — one block
// per bound — not the bootstrap's `cmp`+`cmp`+`or` over materialized bools. A check usually branches to
// the shared panic on violation; the one exception is the unsigned-max-upper shape's sign check, whose
// TRUE edge is IN RANGE (see `RangeCheckEdge`). Signed compares give the same in/out verdict as the
// bootstrap's for every ordinary range; for the `int(N>0 to u64.max)` shape they give the CORRECT
// unsigned verdict, which the bootstrap's RUNTIME check gets wrong (it panics on a bit-63-set value) —
// see `TypeRules.rangeIsUnsignedMaxUpper` / `boundChecks` for why that divergence is deliberate.

// Where a cascade check's TRUE edge (the predicate SATISFIED) goes. Almost every check means
// "out of range past this bound", so its true edge is the shared `panic`. The one exception is
// the unsigned-max-upper shape's FIRST check (`value < 0`), whose satisfied predicate proves the
// value is a huge unsigned that the range ADMITS — its true edge is `inRange`, jumping past the
// remaining checks to the ok block.
enum RangeCheckEdge
	panic
	inRange
end 'RangeCheckEdge'

// One bound of a range check: the predicate that means "the check FIRED past this bound", the bound
// itself, and where a fired check leads. `lt`/`lo` for the lower bound (panic when `value < lo`),
// `gt`/`hi` for the upper — both leading to `panic`. `create` builds the common panic-edge check;
// `createInRange` the one check whose satisfied predicate means IN range (see `RangeCheckEdge`).
type RangeBoundCheck
	export var pred as StdCmpPred
	export var bound as ParsedInt
	export var onTrue as RangeCheckEdge

	static function create(pred StdCmpPred, bound ParsedInt) returns RangeBoundCheck
		return Self{pred: pred, bound: bound, onTrue: RangeCheckEdge.panic}
	end 'create'

	static function createInRange(pred StdCmpPred, bound ParsedInt) returns RangeBoundCheck
		return Self{pred: pred, bound: bound, onTrue: RangeCheckEdge.inRange}
	end 'createInRange'
end 'RangeBoundCheck'

typealias RangeBoundCheckArray = Array with RangeBoundCheck

// A guard's insertion point: the block to split and the `opRefs` position to split it at (ops before
// stay in the head, ops from here on move to the continuation).
type GuardSite
	export var block as IrBlock
	export var position as BlockOpPos

	static function create(block IrBlock, position BlockOpPos) returns GuardSite
		return Self{block: block, position: position}
	end 'create'
end 'GuardSite'

// ⭐ WHICH CHAIN THE EMITTED GUARD EXTENDS — the one thing an alias-named site owes after its split
// that a `return` site does not. Every guard in ONE source block splits that block, so the k-th guard
// must anchor at the end of the (k-1)-th's continuation; anchoring them all at the end of the ORIGINAL
// head instead threads the cascades in REVERSE (see `blockEndGuardSite`). An alias-named site therefore
// moves its block's chain end to its own continuation. A `return` site records nothing: `findRetBlock`
// re-derives its block from the CURRENT `ret` terminator at every site, which a split moves along with
// the terminator, so the chain end is already what it finds.
union GuardChain
	blockChain(blockId BlockId)
	retChain
end 'GuardChain'

// The block whose terminator is `ret(value)` — the return site to guard. `notFound` covers the value
// having been folded away or otherwise not surviving as a `ret` (defensive; a recorded ranged return
// always has one).
union RetBlockLookup
	notFound
	found(block IrBlock)
end 'RetBlockLookup'

// The parameter alias a call-argument site resolves to, once the callee's own `FunctionRangeChecks` is
// in hand: the alias NAME, and the FILE whose declaration of it applies — the CALLEE's, never the
// caller's (`RangedAliasRegistry` resolves a name per declaring file, and a parameter's range is the one
// visible where the function was written).
union ParamAliasLookup
	none
	found(aliasName ByteArray, declFilePath FilePath)
end 'ParamAliasLookup'

// Run the pass over every function that has recorded range-check sites. Functions with none are skipped
// without touching their blocks.
export function insertRangeChecks(project Project, module StdModule)
	logDebug(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "insertRangeChecks: {module.funcCount()} function(s)")
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		// ⭐⭐ **A STDLIB FUNCTION NO PATH FROM `main` REACHES IS SKIPPED, AND THE REASON IS NOT MERELY
		// WASTED WORK — IT IS THE WHITELIST'S BYTE-NEUTRALITY INVARIANT (P1.8 Slice E).** Dead-function
		// elimination drops such a function before the back end, so its guard blocks never reach isel; but
		// this pass registers the guard's PANIC MESSAGE as an `.rdata` constant
		// (`emitPanicBlock` → `registerStringBlob`), and DFE prunes FUNCTIONS, never `.rdata`. The blob
		// therefore survives its own function — and `GlobalDataTable.nextStringId` is ONE counter shared by
		// every rdata prefix, so a surviving blob RENUMBERS every later `__fconst_`/`__str_blob_`/jump-table
		// label in the program.
		//
		// ⚠ MEASURED, and it is exactly what `StdlibLoader`'s "adding a module changes NOTHING for a program
		// that does not use it" forbids: listing `helpers/string/utf16.maxon` — whose
		// `utf16LeadSurrogate`/`utf16TrailSurrogate` each `return` a computed value through a ranged
		// `CodeUnit16` — moved a two-float program from `__fconst_0`/`__fconst_1` to `__fconst_2`/`__fconst_3`
		// and shifted **193 committed goldens** across the suite, for two functions nothing calls.
		//
		// It is the SAME reachability fact `SemanticCheck.checkCalls` skips on and `scanRuntimeUsage` is
		// handed, stated the same way — never "is this whitelisted", which would not outlive the filter. A
		// REACHABLE stdlib function is not in the set, so its guards and its compile-time E3005 are emitted
		// exactly as before, and no user function is ever in it.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **THIS SHUTS ONE DOOR ONTO `nextStringId`, NOT THE CLASS — AND THE SECOND DOOR IS MEASURED
		// AND STILL OPEN** (P1.8 Slice E review). `lowerMaxonToStd` runs one pass EARLIER than this one and
		// is equally pre-elimination, and its `lowerStringLiteral`/`lowerByteStringLiteral` register
		// `.rdata` for the body of ANY function — including a whitelisted stdlib function no path from
		// `main` reaches. MEASURED, by appending one unreachable `export function … returns String` with a
		// single string literal to `stdlib/helpers/string/utf16.maxon` and recompiling a two-float program
		// that mentions no string at all: md5 `765a309c…` → `57d2a6cd…`, the literal's blob taking the
		// front of `.rdata` and displacing both `__fconst_` payloads. So the whitelist's byte-neutrality
		// holds TODAY only because no listed module contains a literal, which is a property of the three
		// files listed and not of the mechanism. The fix belongs with the debt
		// `DeadFunctionElimination`'s header already files — *"it prunes FUNCTIONS, never `.rdata`.
		// Dead-`.rdata` elimination is a separate rung with its own goldens"* — or with a lowering that
		// skips an unreachable stdlib body the way this pass does; either is a rung, not a line.
		if project.stdlibFacts.unreachable.contains(func.name) 'notInTheProgram'
			continue
		end 'notInTheProgram'
		if not project.rangeChecks.contains(func.name) 'noChecks'
			continue
		end 'noChecks'
		let checks = try project.rangeChecks.get(func.name) otherwise panic("insertRangeChecks: rangeChecks.contains said yes but get failed for '{String.from(func.name)}'")
		var emitter = RangeCheckEmitter.create(project, module: module, func: func)
		emitter.run(checks)
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'insertRangeChecks'

// The `guardChainTail` an emitter starts with, SHARED and never mutated — `blockEndGuardSite` REPLACES
// the field with a freshly built index rather than filling this one.
//
// ⚠ It is shared for the reason `Parser`'s `sharedEmptyInstanceAliasNames` is: an emitter is built for
// every function carrying ANY range-check side data, which includes every function that merely passes a
// constant argument or constructs a generic instance, so an unconditional `ValueIdArray.create()` in
// `create` is one allocation per such function across the whole program. MEASURED on the scale ladder:
// as a per-emitter `create()` it cost +45 / +66 / +108 / +192 / +360 / +696 allocations across the six
// rungs — a linear cost, but a real one, paid entirely by functions that never emit a guard. Only ONE
// function in the whole generated corpus ever builds a real index (`scaleCoverCast`).
let sharedEmptyGuardChainTail = ValueIdArray.create()

// Per-function emitter: owns the module + function it rewrites and the fresh-ValueId counter its guard
// ops draw from (seeded past every id the function already uses).
type RangeCheckEmitter
	var project as Project
	var module as StdModule
	var func as IrFunction
	var nextId as ValueId
	// The function's compile-time constants, PARTITIONED BY DOMAIN: an integer `const`'s payload is a
	// number, a float `const`'s is an IEEE-754 bit pattern, and the two must never be read as each
	// other. Filled by `collectConsts` at the top of `run`; a site consults the map that matches its
	// alias's domain, so a domain mismatch simply is not a literal and falls through to the runtime
	// guard (which is also what the bootstrap does — see `needsRuntimeGuard`).
	var intConsts as ConstValueMap
	var floatConsts as ConstValueMap
	// Where each of this function's blocks currently ENDS — a blockRef index (+1, `buildBlockIdIndex`'s
	// sentinel encoding) per block id. It starts as the plain block-id => blockRef index (every block is
	// its own chain end), and each guard MOVES its site block's entry to the continuation the split
	// minted, so the next guard in that source block lands after it rather than before it. Starts as the
	// SHARED empty one and is REPLACED by a real index on first use — see `sharedEmptyGuardChainTail`
	// and `blockEndGuardSite`.
	var guardChainTail as ValueIdArray

	static function create(project Project, module StdModule, func IrFunction) returns RangeCheckEmitter
		return Self{project: project, module: module, func: func, nextId: nextFreeValueId(module, func: func), intConsts: ConstValueMap.create(), floatConsts: ConstValueMap.create(), guardChainTail: sharedEmptyGuardChainTail}
	end 'create'

	// A fresh ValueId for a guard op. The colored register file / dense value columns downstream grow to
	// fit the highest id, so a monotonically increasing id past the function's current maximum is all
	// that is required.
	function mintId() returns ValueId
		let id = self.nextId
		self.nextId = self.nextId + 1
		return id
	end 'mintId'

	// Process one function's sites. The alias-named sites FIRST (they split blocks and move terminators
	// onto new continuation blocks), then the call-argument sites — which emit nothing, so their order is
	// free — then returns (re-found from the CURRENT block structure, since a split may have moved a `ret`
	// terminator). The literal/const map is built ONCE over the original ops — the values a site checks are
	// original, never the guard consts this pass appends afterward.
	function run(checks FunctionRangeChecks)
		self.collectConsts()
		for site in checks.sites 'eachSite'
			self.processSite(site, aliasFilePath: checks.filePath, filePath: checks.filePath)
		end 'eachSite'
		for argSite in checks.argSites 'eachArgSite'
			self.processArgSite(argSite, filePath: checks.filePath)
		end 'eachArgSite'
		if not checks.returnAliasName.isEmpty() 'rangedReturn'
			self.processReturnSites(checks.returnAliasName, sites: checks.returnSites, filePath: checks.filePath)
		end 'rangedReturn'
	end 'run'

	// ---- alias-named sites (cast, struct-literal field, field store, field default, array element) ----

	// ⭐ **TWO FILES, AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME QUESTION.** `aliasFilePath` picks WHICH declaration of the
	// name applies — a file's own `Limit` governs it even where another file declares that name over a
	// wider range (`RangedAliasRegistry`). `filePath` is where the DIAGNOSTIC and the runtime panic message
	// point. They are one file at every door the parser resolved for itself; they DIFFER at a call
	// argument, where the parameter's range is the CALLEE's to declare and the error belongs to the line
	// that wrote the argument. Conflated, `utf16LeadSurrogate(1000000000)` reported the caller's line and
	// column against the STDLIB's path — a position that names a `typealias` declaration, in a file the
	// program never wrote a call in. (Measured, on `string-views`.)
	//
	// `undeclared` — a builtin like `ExitCode`, or an alias no loaded file declares — is the "not a
	// checkable range" case and guards nothing.
	function processSite(site RangeCheckSite, aliasFilePath FilePath, filePath FilePath)
		match self.project.typeAliases.lookup(site.aliasName, readerFilePath: aliasFilePath) 'bounds'
			undeclared then return
			declared(alias) then self.guardSite(site, alias: alias, filePath: filePath)
		end 'bounds'
	end 'processSite'

	// ⚠ **`needsRuntimeGuard` IS ASKED FIRST AND UNCONDITIONALLY, INCLUDING FOR A SITE THAT WILL NEVER
	// EMIT ONE**, because raising the compile-time E3005 for a folded literal is what it does on the way to
	// its answer. Both halves of the rule are one decision (`range-check-panic.md` says so outright), and
	// splitting them so a `compileTimeOnly` site could skip the call would be two deciders that can drift.
	// The `guard` field then decides only whether the RUNTIME half is emitted.
	function guardSite(site RangeCheckSite, alias RangedTypeAlias, filePath FilePath)
		if not self.needsRuntimeGuard(site.value, alias: alias, aliasName: site.aliasName, line: site.line, column: site.column, filePath: filePath) 'noGuard'
			return
		end 'noGuard'
		if site.guard == RangeCheckGuard.compileTimeOnly 'compileTimeHalfOnly'
			return
		end 'compileTimeHalfOnly'
		self.emitGuardAt(self.blockEndGuardSite(site), value: site.value, alias: alias, message: self.rangeCheckMessage(filePath, line: site.line, aliasName: site.aliasName), chain: GuardChain.blockChain(site.block))
	end 'guardSite'

	// ---- call-argument sites ----

	// ⭐ **THE ONE SITE KIND WHOSE ALIAS IS RESOLVED HERE RATHER THAN AT THE PARSE.** A call argument's
	// declared type belongs to the callee, which the caller's file may never have seen (see
	// `RangeCheckArgSite`); this pass runs once, after every file has merged, so the callee's own record is
	// in hand. Resolved, it becomes an ordinary `compileTimeOnly` site and takes the shared path above —
	// there is no second decision anywhere for a call argument.
	//
	// ⚠ The alias reader file is the CALLEE's, never this function's: the range a parameter was declared
	// with is the one visible where the FUNCTION was written. The diagnostic's file stays this function's
	// — see `processSite`.
	function processArgSite(site RangeCheckArgSite, filePath FilePath)
		match self.paramAliasOf(site) 'p'
			none then return
			found(aliasName, declFilePath) then self.processSite(RangeCheckSite.create(site.value, aliasName: aliasName, block: site.block, guard: RangeCheckGuard.compileTimeOnly, line: site.line, column: site.column), aliasFilePath: declFilePath, filePath: filePath)
		end 'p'
	end 'processArgSite'

	// The ranged-alias name the argument's PARAMETER was declared with, plus the callee's file. `none`
	// covers every ordinary call: a callee with no ranged parameter at all files no `paramAliasNames`, an
	// argument landing on an unranged position carries `EmptySourceText`, and a malformed call (bad arity,
	// unknown label) slots past the end — which SemanticCheck reports against the real registry, and which
	// is not this pass's to diagnose.
	function paramAliasOf(site RangeCheckArgSite) returns ParamAliasLookup
		let calleeChecks = try self.project.rangeChecks.get(site.callee) otherwise return ParamAliasLookup.none
		if calleeChecks.paramAliasNames.isEmpty() 'noRangedParam'
			return ParamAliasLookup.none
		end 'noRangedParam'

		// A `rangeChecks` entry and a `funcSignatures` entry are written by the SAME `parseFunction`, one
		// statement apart and under the same name, so a callee present in one and absent from the other is a
		// broken invariant rather than a program the user could write.
		let signature = try self.project.funcSignatures.get(site.callee) otherwise panic("paramAliasOf: '{String.from(site.callee)}' has range-check data but no FuncSignature — parseFunction records both, keyed by the same name")
		// `argSlotPosition` is the ONE label->position mapping in the compiler; asking it here is what keeps
		// a labelled argument checked against the parameter it actually lands on rather than the one it
		// happens to sit beside.
		let aliasName = try calleeChecks.paramAliasNames.get(argSlotPosition(signature.paramNames, argIndex: site.argIndex, label: site.label)) otherwise return ParamAliasLookup.none
		if aliasName.isEmpty() 'positionNotRanged'
			return ParamAliasLookup.none
		end 'positionNotRanged'

		return ParamAliasLookup.found(aliasName, declFilePath: calleeChecks.filePath)
	end 'paramAliasOf'

	// ---- return sites ----

	// `filePath` picks the alias, exactly as it does for a cast — a ranged `return` is checked against
	// the range declared where the FUNCTION was written.
	function processReturnSites(returnAliasName ByteArray, sites RangeCheckReturnSiteArray, filePath FilePath)
		match self.project.typeAliases.lookup(returnAliasName, readerFilePath: filePath) 'bounds'
			undeclared then return
			declared(alias) then self.guardReturns(alias, aliasName: returnAliasName, sites: sites, filePath: filePath)
		end 'bounds'
	end 'processReturnSites'

	function guardReturns(alias RangedTypeAlias, aliasName ByteArray, sites RangeCheckReturnSiteArray, filePath FilePath)
		for site in sites 'eachSite'
			if not self.needsRuntimeGuard(site.value, alias: alias, aliasName: aliasName, line: site.line, column: site.column, filePath: filePath) 'noGuard'
				continue
			end 'noGuard'
			match self.findRetBlock(site.value) 'ret'
				notFound then break 'ret'
				found(retBlock) then self.emitGuardAt(GuardSite.create(retBlock, position: retBlock.opRefs.count()), value: site.value, alias: alias, message: self.rangeCheckMessage(filePath, line: site.line, aliasName: aliasName), chain: GuardChain.retChain)
			end 'ret'
		end 'eachSite'
	end 'guardReturns'

	// ---- the shared decision ----

	// Whether a site needs a RUNTIME guard. A full-range alias never does; a literal of the alias's OWN
	// domain never does (its out-of-range use is reported here as a compile-time E3005, its in-range use
	// needs no code); everything else does.
	//
	// ⚠ THE DOMAIN TEST IS PART OF THE LITERAL TEST, not decoration. `collectConsts` records every
	// `const`'s payload with no regard for its type, and a FLOAT const's payload is an IEEE-754 bit
	// pattern — so a float const reaching an integer alias (or the reverse) would be compared as a
	// number it is not. Falling through to the runtime guard for a domain mismatch is also what the
	// bootstrap does: `ValidateAndEmitRangeCheck` compile-time-checks a float ranged type only against a
	// FLOAT literal and emits a runtime check otherwise.
	function needsRuntimeGuard(value ValueId, alias RangedTypeAlias, aliasName ByteArray, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber, filePath FilePath) returns bool
		let isFloat = aliasIsFloat(alias)
		if rangeIsFull(alias.low, high: alias.high, isFloat: isFloat) 'full'
			return false
		end 'full'

		let literals = self.floatConsts if isFloat else self.intConsts
		if literals.contains(value) 'literal'
			let v = literals.constantOf(value)
			if not literalInRange(v, low: alias.low, high: alias.high, isFloat: isFloat) 'outOfRange'
				reportPositioned(self.project, code: ErrorCode.typeMismatch, message: literalOutOfRangeMessage(v, aliasName: String.from(aliasName), low: alias.low, high: alias.high, isFloat: isFloat), line: line, column: column, filePath: filePath)
			end 'outOfRange'
			return false
		end 'literal'

		return true
	end 'needsRuntimeGuard'

	// Split the site's block, emit the panic block, thread the compare cascade between them, and extend
	// `chain` onto the continuation so the NEXT guard on this path lands after this one (see `GuardChain`
	// for why only an alias-named site has a chain to extend).
	function emitGuardAt(site GuardSite, value ValueId, alias RangedTypeAlias, message ByteArray, chain GuardChain)
		let isFloat = aliasIsFloat(alias)
		let checks = boundChecks(alias.low, high: alias.high, isFloat: isFloat)
		if checks.isEmpty() 'noBoundNeeded'
			// Both bounds elide (a signed range whose only stored bound is a negative upper) — nothing to
			// guard. Matches the bootstrap's both-elided path, which returns the value unchecked. Nothing was
			// split, so the chain end is where it was.
			return
		end 'noBoundNeeded'

		let ok = self.splitBlock(site.block, position: site.position)
		// ⚠ **BEFORE `emitPanicBlock`, AND THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING** — see `extendGuardChain`.
		match chain 'chain'
			blockChain(siteBlockId) then self.extendGuardChain(siteBlockId, continuation: ok)
			retChain then break 'chain'
		end 'chain'
		let panicBlock = self.emitPanicBlock(message)
		self.emitCascade(site.block, value: value, checks: checks, panicId: panicBlock.id, okId: ok.id, isFloat: isFloat)
	end 'emitGuardAt'

	// Move `siteBlockId`'s chain end onto the continuation `splitBlock` just made, so the next guard in
	// that source block lands after this one instead of before it.
	//
	// ⚠ **THIS MUST RUN BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE APPENDS A BLOCK.** The continuation's own blockRef is
	// recoverable only as "the last entry `addBlock` registered", and `emitPanicBlock` plus the cascade's
	// `__rc_chk` blocks append more the moment they run. So it is read here — and CHECKED against the
	// block the split actually returned, not assumed: a later reordering that slipped an append in
	// between would chain the NEXT guard onto the panic block, and no test would show that as anything
	// but wrong code at some unrelated site.
	function extendGuardChain(siteBlockId BlockId, continuation IrBlock)
		let okRef = try self.func.blockRefs.get(self.func.blockRefs.count() - 1) otherwise panic("extendGuardChain: `addBlock` made the continuation of '{String.from(self.func.name)}' but registered no blockRef for it")
		if self.module.getBlock(okRef).id != continuation.id 'notTheContinuation'
			panic("extendGuardChain: the last block registered in '{String.from(self.func.name)}' is not the continuation `splitBlock` returned — a block was appended between the split and this record, so the chain would advance onto the wrong block")
		end 'notTheContinuation'

		try self.guardChainTail.set(siteBlockId, value: okRef + 1) otherwise panic("extendGuardChain: guard site block id {siteBlockId} is outside the function's block-id space, which `blockEndGuardSite` already resolved it within")
	end 'extendGuardChain'

	// Split `head` at `position`: ops before it stay in `head`; ops from it on, plus `head`'s
	// terminator / conditional branch / outgoing edges, move to a fresh continuation block returned
	// here. `head` is left un-terminated for the cascade to finish.
	function splitBlock(head IrBlock, position BlockOpPos) returns IrBlock
		var ok = self.module.addBlock(self.func, label: b"__rc_ok")
		// A mutable alias to the head block (a parameter binding is immutable; field writes need a `var`).
		// It is the SAME heap object — IrBlock is a reference type — so these writes land on the real block.
		var headBlock = head

		var headOps = OpIndexArray.create()
		var tailOps = OpIndexArray.create()
		for i in 0 upto headBlock.opRefs.count() 'partition'
			let opIdx = try headBlock.opRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("splitBlock: opRefs.get OOB at {i}")
			if i < position 'inHead'
				headOps.push(opIdx)
			end 'inHead' else 'inTail'
				tailOps.push(opIdx)
			end 'inTail'
		end 'partition'

		ok.opRefs = tailOps
		ok.terminator = headBlock.terminator
		ok.condBranch = headBlock.condBranch
		ok.branchEdges = headBlock.branchEdges

		headBlock.opRefs = headOps
		headBlock.terminator = Terminator.unset
		headBlock.condBranch = CondBranch.none
		headBlock.branchEdges = BranchEdgeArray.create()
		return ok
	end 'splitBlock'

	// The panic block: `osRangePanic <message-label>`, then a dead return (the panic never returns, but
	// every block must terminate). The message is interned as a NUL-terminated `.rdata` cstr blob — the
	// same immortal-rdata path a String literal's blob uses, deduped by bytes — and `osRangePanic`
	// carries its label to the backend, which loads the address into the ABI's first-arg register and
	// calls `mrt_panic`.
	function emitPanicBlock(message ByteArray) returns IrBlock
		var panicBlock = self.module.addBlock(self.func, label: b"__rc_panic")
		let blobLabel = registerStringBlob(self.project.globalData, bytes: message)
		self.module.appendOp(panicBlock, op: StdOp.osRangePanic(blobLabel.toByteArray()))
		self.emitDeadReturn(panicBlock)
		return panicBlock
	end 'emitPanicBlock'

	// The unreachable terminator after `mrt_panic`: a `retVoid` for a void function, else a `ret` of a
	// zero constant in the return type's register file (an f64 zero for a float return, an i64 zero
	// otherwise — the same class rule `emitZeroConstOfReturnType` follows so the backend's return move
	// never crosses register files). Mirrors the parser's `emitDeadReturn` after `panicAbort`.
	function emitDeadReturn(block IrBlock)
		if stdReturnTypeIsVoid(self.func.returnType) 'void'
			self.module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.retVoid)
			return
		end 'void'
		let zero = self.mintId()
		let zeroType = StdType.f64 if stdReturnTypeIsFloat(self.func.returnType) else StdType.i64
		self.module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(zero, value: 0, valueType: zeroType))
		self.module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.ret(zero))
	end 'emitDeadReturn'

	// Emit the compare cascade, `head` first: each check is `const bound; cmp value <pred> bound;
	// condBranch (predicate TRUE) -> trueTarget, (FALSE) -> next`. The TRUE target is `panicId` for an
	// out-of-range check and `okId` for an in-range one (see `RangeCheckEdge`); the FALSE edge falls
	// through to the next check, or to `okId` for the last one. The `const` is left as a register
	// operand — `foldConstOperands`, which runs after this pass, folds it into the `cmp`'s immediate.
	function emitCascade(head IrBlock, value ValueId, checks RangeBoundCheckArray, panicId BlockId, okId BlockId, isFloat bool)
		// A float alias's bounds are f64 bit patterns and its guarded value lives in an XMM register, so
		// BOTH the materialized bound and the compare carry the f64 type. `foldConstOperands` refuses to
		// fold a float compare's operand into an immediate (there is no SSE immediate form), so the
		// `const` correctly stays a register operand here where an integer one would be folded away.
		let operandType = StdType.f64 if isFloat else StdType.i64
		var current = head
		let lastIndex = checks.count() - 1
		for i in 0 upto checks.count() 'eachCheck'
			let check = try checks.get(i) otherwise panic("emitCascade: checks.get OOB at {i}")
			let boundId = self.mintId()
			self.module.appendOp(current, op: StdOp.const(boundId, value: check.bound, valueType: operandType))
			let condId = self.mintId()
			self.module.appendOp(current, op: StdOp.cmp(condId, lhs: value, rhs: boundId, pred: check.pred, operandType: operandType))

			let trueTarget = okId if check.onTrue == RangeCheckEdge.inRange else panicId
			if i == lastIndex 'lastCheck'
				self.module.setTerminator(current, op: StdOp.condBranch(condId, thenBlockId: trueTarget, elseBlockId: okId))
			end 'lastCheck' else 'moreChecks'
				let next = self.module.addBlock(self.func, label: b"__rc_chk")
				self.module.setTerminator(current, op: StdOp.condBranch(condId, thenBlockId: trueTarget, elseBlockId: next.id))
				current = next
			end 'moreChecks'
		end 'eachCheck'
	end 'emitCascade'

	// Where a guard goes: at the END of the block the value was CHECKED in (before that block's
	// terminator), so the guard runs on the path the site is on and NOWHERE else — a cast in an unselected
	// ternary arm / if-branch does not fire it. An int->int cast emits no Std op to anchor to, so the block
	// is the one the parser recorded (`site.block`, its on-the-fly-SSA current block at the `as`), and the
	// checked value is available there (a parameter, a value from a dominating block, or one defined earlier
	// in this block — the site reads it, so it is already defined). For a straight-line function the site's
	// block IS entry, so end-of-body equals the post-param point the older anchoring used — those goldens
	// are unchanged; only a site inside a branch moves.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **"THE END OF THE BLOCK" MOVES AS SOON AS THE FIRST GUARD SPLITS IT, AND RESOLVING EVERY SITE
	// TO THE ORIGINAL HEAD IS TWO BUGS AT ONCE.** N sites in one source block all name that block's id.
	// Each split moves the head's terminator onto a fresh continuation and leaves the new cascade in the
	// head — so resolving the (k+1)-th site to the head again puts its cascade BEFORE the k-th's branch
	// and pushes that branch one block further down. Chained that way the guards RUN IN REVERSE SOURCE
	// ORDER (measured: two out-of-range casts on consecutive lines panicked on the SECOND), and — the
	// costlier half — all N `cmp` results are DEFINED in the head while each is READ by the terminator of
	// a different block k levels down. `recordFusableCompare` fuses a compare only when it is the last
	// flag-clobbering op of the branch's OWN block, so N-1 of them lose their fusion, materialize through
	// `setcc`, and sit in a register across the whole chain: N-1 compiler-introduced values live at one
	// point, which is what RULE 3 (RegisterPressureDiagnostic) forbids outright. At N=16 that overflowed
	// the 14-GPR file and the RULE 3 backstop panicked, on a program with nothing wrong with it.
	//
	// So the index is a CHAIN END, not a block identity: a guard moves its site block's entry to its own
	// continuation, the (k+1)-th cascade lands at the end of the k-th's `__rc_ok`, and every `cmp` is
	// again the last op before the branch that reads it — fused, holding no register, invisible to the
	// allocator. Guards then also fire in source order, which is the order a reader expects the FIRST
	// out-of-range value to be reported in.
	//
	// ⚠ **END-OF-BLOCK IS A PATH ANCHOR, NOT A STATEMENT ANCHOR, and a FIELD STORE is where that shows.**
	// The bootstrap emits a store's guard immediately before the store; here it lands at the end of the
	// store's block, so a side effect written between the two runs before the panic does. The verdict is
	// identical either way — a guard tests a VALUE, and the value is the same number at both points, so the
	// program panics on exactly the same inputs — and the panic is fatal, so nothing observes the stored
	// value afterwards; what can differ is the ORDER of output emitted between the store and the block's
	// end. Anchoring on the store itself would need a Maxon-tier op position, and positions do not survive
	// `lowerMaxonToStd`'s 1:N desugaring — the reason this pass anchors on a BLOCK in the first place.
	// ⚠ **THIS RESOLVES THROUGH AN INDEX, NOT `IrModule.getBlockByIdIn`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A GROWTH
	// CURVE.** `getBlockByIdIn` is a LINEAR SCAN of `func.blockRefs`; called once per guard site it made
	// this pass **O(sites × blocks)** — and worse than the plain product, because every guard this pass
	// emits APPENDS TWO BLOCKS to the very set the next site's scan walks (`__rc_ok`, `__rc_panic`), so the
	// k-th site scanned a set the previous k-1 had grown. `buildBlockIdIndex`/`blockById` are the cure that
	// already existed for exactly this shape one pass over: `pruneDeadBlockArgs` introduced them because
	// resolving an edge per call "would be O(edges × blocks), the shape the allocator's data-representation
	// contract exists to forbid", and `elimTrivialBlockArgs` shares them rather than keeping a second copy.
	// This is the third user of the same index over the same id space, not a fourth mechanism.
	//
	// ⚠ **LAZY, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER THAN THRIFT.** A `rangeChecks` entry is filed for a
	// function that merely passes a CONSTANT ARGUMENT to any declared callee, or constructs a generic
	// instance (`Parser.commitRangeChecks`) — so the overwhelming majority of entries reach this pass with
	// no RUNTIME site at all, and a call-argument site is `compileTimeOnly` and never arrives here. Building
	// the index in `create` would hand every one of those functions a fresh `func.nextBlockId`-sized array
	// it never reads, turning a cost this pass does not currently pay into one it pays program-wide — and
	// allocations are the trend log's exact column, so that would read as a regression on every future row.
	//
	// The index is safe to hold across the splits and block appends that follow: it stores blockRef
	// INDICES, not `IrBlock` aliases (`blockById` re-fetches from `module.blocks` on every call, so no
	// alias goes stale), a split MUTATES its head block in place and never moves it, and every id a site
	// names was recorded by the parser and therefore predates the pass. Its ROWS are only ever the
	// parser's block ids — a continuation is written as a VALUE at its site block's row and never gets a
	// row of its own — so it stays exactly `func.nextBlockId` wide however many blocks the pass appends.
	function blockEndGuardSite(site RangeCheckSite) returns GuardSite
		if self.guardChainTail.isEmpty() 'firstRuntimeGuard'
			self.guardChainTail = buildBlockIdIndex(self.module, func: self.func)
		end 'firstRuntimeGuard'
		let block = blockById(self.module, refByBlockId: self.guardChainTail, id: site.block, from: self.func.name)
		return GuardSite.create(block, position: block.opRefs.count())
	end 'blockEndGuardSite'

	// The block whose terminator is `ret(value)`.
	function findRetBlock(value ValueId) returns RetBlockLookup
		for blockRef in self.func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = self.module.getBlock(blockRef)
			if block.hasTerminatorOp() 'hasTerm'
				let term = try self.module.ops.get(block.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("findRetBlock: terminatorIndex not in module.ops")
				if retMatchesValue(term, target: value) 'match'
					return RetBlockLookup.found(block)
				end 'match'
			end 'hasTerm'
		end 'eachBlock'
		return RetBlockLookup.notFound
	end 'findRetBlock'

	// ---- the panic message ----

	// The runtime panic string, baked at compile time: `panic at <file>:<line>: Range check failed:
	// value outside typealias '<Alias>'\n`. `<file>` is the source BASENAME (the bootstrap's
	// `Path.GetFileName`), `<line>` the `as`/`return` source line. `mrt_panic` prints this verbatim,
	// then the `Stack trace:` block, then exits 1.
	function rangeCheckMessage(filePath FilePath, line LineNumber, aliasName ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		return "panic at {baseName(filePath.toString())}:{line}: Range check failed: value outside typealias '{String.from(aliasName)}'\n".toByteArray()
	end 'rangeCheckMessage'

	// Map each `const` result value id to its constant — the literals whose out-of-range use is the
	// compile-time E3005 and whose in-range use needs no runtime code. Built once per function over the
	// pre-guard ops, and PARTITIONED BY THE CONST'S OWN TYPE (see the `intConsts`/`floatConsts` fields):
	// the payloads are numbers on one side and IEEE-754 bit patterns on the other, and nothing
	// downstream could tell them apart from the payload alone.
	function collectConsts()
		for opIdx in self.module.liveOpIndices(self.func) 'eachOp'
			let op = try self.module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("collectConsts: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops")
			match op 'c'
				const(resultId, value, valueType) then self.recordConst(resultId, value: value, valueType: valueType)
				// A NEGATED INTEGER literal (`-5 as Positive`) survives lowering as `unaryOp neg` over a
				// `const`, not a folded `const`, so the E3005 literal check would miss it and emit a runtime
				// guard where the spec expects a COMPILE error. Fold it here — the `const` operand is already
				// recorded (it is emitted before the `neg`, and ops are walked in order). Only `neg` folds;
				// every other unary (a domain conversion, a bitwise `not`) is left to the runtime path.
				//
				// A negated FLOAT literal needs nothing here: the parser folds `-<float literal>` into a
				// single literal carrying the sign-flipped bits (`parseNegatedNumericLiteral`), so it arrives
				// as a plain `const` — and `neg` is the INTEGER negate, which is why this stays int-only.
				binOp then break 'c'
				unaryOp(resultId, operand, opcode, _) then recordConstNeg(self.intConsts, resultId: resultId, operand: operand, opcode: opcode)
				cmp to osFreePages then break 'c'
				switch then break 'c'
				// A witness call's result is a call return, not a foldable constant — nothing to record, and
				// neither of the throwing form's two results is one either.
				witnessCall then break 'c'
				witnessTryCall then break 'c'
				// P1.5 dogfood slice 1a: the IOCP ops produce no ranged-typealias value to range-check.
				osPipeCreate to osWriteFile then break 'c'
				// The wall-clock read produces no ranged-typealias value to range-check.
				osReadWallClock then break 'c'
			end 'c'
		end 'eachOp'
	end 'collectConsts'

	function recordConst(resultId ValueId, value ParsedInt, valueType StdType)
		if stdTypeIsFloat(valueType) 'float'
			self.floatConsts.upsert(resultId, value: value)
			return
		end 'float'
		self.intConsts.upsert(resultId, value: value)
	end 'recordConst'
end 'RangeCheckEmitter'

// The i64 the sign check compares against: a value `< 0` (signed) has bit 63 set, which for the
// unsigned-max upper shape proves it is a huge unsigned the range ADMITS. Named so the sign
// boundary is not a bare `0` sitting beside the range's own bounds.
let SignBoundary = 0 as ParsedInt

// The bound checks a `[low, high]` range needs, in cascade order. Empty when the range is unbounded
// on both sides in signed space — which only the full-range case reaches before this (elided
// earlier), or a signed range with a negative upper (matched to the bootstrap's both-elided path).
// See TypeRules' `rangeNeedsLowerCheck` / `rangeNeedsUpperCheck`.
function boundChecks(low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt, isFloat bool) returns RangeBoundCheckArray
	var checks = RangeBoundCheckArray.create()

	// ⭐ THE UNSIGNED-MAX UPPER SHAPE — `int(N to u64.max)` over a non-negative low bound. Signed
	// compares cannot express its upper (it rides as `-1`), so out-of-range is `value >= 0 AND
	// value < low`: a negative (bit-63-set) value is a huge unsigned IN range, a non-negative value
	// below `low` is out. The cascade is `value < 0 → in range` then `value < low → panic`, which the
	// backend fuses to `cmp value,0; jl ok; cmp value,low; jl panic; jmp ok`. The full-unsigned
	// `int(0 to u64.max)` (low == 0) never reaches here — `rangeIsFull` elides it in
	// `needsRuntimeGuard` — so the only shape this arm serves is a POSITIVE low bound.
	//
	// ⚠ A FLOAT IS NEVER ELIGIBLE, and this is v1's one behavioural divergence worth carrying over
	// verbatim (InsertRangeChecks.maxon:141, `and not typeInfo.isFloat`). The shape reinterprets a
	// signed bound as an unsigned one; IEEE-754 ordering is sign-and-magnitude and matches neither,
	// so a float bound whose pattern happens to equal -1 (which is a NaN, not `u64.max`) must take
	// the ordinary two-bound path.
	if not isFloat and rangeIsUnsignedMaxUpper(low, high: high) 'unsignedMaxUpper'
		checks.push(RangeBoundCheck.createInRange(StdCmpPred.lt, bound: SignBoundary))
		checks.push(RangeBoundCheck.create(StdCmpPred.lt, bound: low))
		return checks
	end 'unsignedMaxUpper'

	// ⚠ A NaN PASSES a float guard, and that is the MEASURED behaviour of both compilers rather than an
	// oversight here. `lt`/`gt` lower to the NaN-correct SWAPPED `ucomisd`+`ja` form, which answers
	// FALSE for an unordered compare — so neither bound fires and the value is admitted. The bootstrap
	// admits it too: `sqrt(-1.0) as Pct` runs to completion under both, verified by running. Rejecting a
	// NaN would need a check whose FALSE edge is the panic (`value >= low` failing on unordered), which
	// the cascade deliberately does not have, and would DIVERGE from the oracle.
	if rangeNeedsLowerCheck(low, isFloat: isFloat) 'lower'
		checks.push(RangeBoundCheck.create(StdCmpPred.lt, bound: low))
	end 'lower'
	if rangeNeedsUpperCheck(high, isFloat: isFloat) 'upper'
		checks.push(RangeBoundCheck.create(StdCmpPred.gt, bound: high))
	end 'upper'
	return checks
end 'boundChecks'

// Record a negated-constant result: `neg` of a recorded constant `operand` is `-operand`. Any other
// unary opcode is not an integer constant this check reads, so nothing is recorded.
function recordConstNeg(constMap ConstValueMap, resultId ValueId, operand ValueId, opcode StdUnaryOpcode)
	if opcode != StdUnaryOpcode.neg 'notNeg'
		return
	end 'notNeg'
	if not constMap.contains(operand) 'operandNotConst'
		return
	end 'operandNotConst'
	constMap.upsert(resultId, value: 0 - constMap.constantOf(operand))
end 'recordConstNeg'

// Does `term` return exactly `target`?
function retMatchesValue(term StdOp, target ValueId) returns bool
	return match term 'term'
		const to branch gives false
		ret(retValId) gives retValId == target
		param to osFreePages gives false
		// The jump table is a branch terminator, not a `ret` — it returns no value.
		switch gives false
		// A witness call is a body op, not a `ret` — it is not the return whose value this checks. Nor is the
		// throwing form: it is a body op with two results, neither of them a return terminator.
		witnessCall gives false
		witnessTryCall gives false
		// P1.5 dogfood slice 1a: an IOCP op is a body op, never a `ret`.
		osPipeCreate to osWriteFile gives false
		// The wall-clock read is a body op, never a `ret`.
		osReadWallClock gives false
	end 'term'
end 'retMatchesValue'

// The next unused ValueId in the function: one past the highest id any op DEFINES or any block-arg
// carries. Scanning defs (and phi results) suffices — dense SSA numbers every value once, so the
// maximum def id is the maximum id, and a use can only name an id that some def already introduced.
function nextFreeValueId(module StdModule, func IrFunction) returns ValueId
	var maxId = 0 as ValueId
	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("nextFreeValueId: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
		for arg in block.blockArgs 'eachArg'
			maxId = maxValueId(maxId, other: arg.valueId)
		end 'eachArg'
	end 'eachBlock'
	for opIdx in module.liveOpIndices(func) 'eachOp'
		let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("nextFreeValueId: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops")
		if stdOpDefinesValue(op) 'defines'
			maxId = maxValueId(maxId, other: stdOpDefinedIdOrZero(op))
		end 'defines'
	end 'eachOp'
	return maxId + 1
end 'nextFreeValueId'

function maxValueId(a ValueId, other ValueId) returns ValueId
	return a if a >= other else other
end 'maxValueId'

// True iff `op` DEFINES a value (has a result). The `false` set is the terminators / stores / effect
// ops that define nothing. Exhaustive over StdOp, matching `collectStdOpUses`'s discipline — a new
// variant is a compile error here rather than a silently-missed def.
function stdOpDefinesValue(op StdOp) returns bool
	return match op 'd'
		const to cmpImm gives true
		div to mod gives true
		condBranch to branch gives false
		ret gives false
		param gives true
		call gives true
		retVoid gives false
		globalAddr to loadIndirect gives true
		storeIndirect gives false
		rdataAddr gives true
		tryCall gives true
		errorReturn to osExit gives false
		osAllocPages gives true
		osWriteStdout gives false
		funcAddr gives true
		callIndirect gives true
		// P1.5 subprocess: only `osWaitHandle` (the `WaitForSingleObject` poll) defines a value; the other four
		// consume their `BOOL` via memory or ignore it (see StdDialect's ⭐ note).
		osWaitHandle gives true
		osRangePanic to osCloseHandle gives false
		// `osFreePages` (P1.5-B1a′) defines no value; it sits past `osWaitHandle` in the union, so its own arm.
		osFreePages gives false
		// The jump table defines no value — a terminator branches, it does not produce a result.
		switch gives false
		// A witness call DEFINES its result (the dispatched method's return value), like `callIndirect`. The
		// throwing form defines that result AND an error flag — reported here as `tryCall`'s two are, by its
		// PRIMARY alone, since this pair of readers models one def per op.
		witnessCall gives true
		witnessTryCall gives true
		// P1.5 dogfood slice 1a: the FIVE value-defining IOCP ops (iocp/event/thread handles, the read BOOL, the
		// last-error code) capture RAX; osPipeCreate/osLock*/osProcessSpawnInherit define nothing (see StdDialect).
		osPipeCreate gives false
		osIocpCreate to osThreadCreate gives true
		osLockInit to osLockLeave gives false
		osReadOverlapped to osLastError gives true
		osProcessSpawnInherit gives false
		osCancelIo gives false
		osGetPid gives true
		// P1.5 dogfood slice 1b: osWriteFile captures WriteFile's BOOL (a value); osPipeCreateOutbound defines nothing.
		osPipeCreateOutbound gives false
		osWriteFile gives true
		// The wall-clock read defines nothing — its answer lands in the caller's out-param word.
		osReadWallClock gives false
	end 'd'
end 'stdOpDefinesValue'

// The ValueId `op` defines, or 0 for a non-defining op (safe filler for a MAX, never a real answer read
// on its own — every caller gates on `stdOpDefinesValue`). Exhaustive over StdOp.
function stdOpDefinedIdOrZero(op StdOp) returns ValueId
	return match op 'r'
		const(resultId, _, _) gives resultId
		binOp(resultId, _, _, _, _) gives resultId
		unaryOp(resultId, _, _, _) gives resultId
		cmp(resultId, _, _, _, _) gives resultId
		binOpImm(resultId, _, _, _) gives resultId
		cmpImm(resultId, _, _, _) gives resultId
		div(resultId, _, _) gives resultId
		mod(resultId, _, _) gives resultId
		condBranch to branch gives 0
		ret gives 0
		param(resultId, _, _) gives resultId
		call(resultId, _, _) gives resultId
		retVoid gives 0
		globalAddr(resultId, _) gives resultId
		loadIndirect(resultId, _, _, _) gives resultId
		storeIndirect gives 0
		rdataAddr(resultId, _) gives resultId
		tryCall(resultId, _, _, _) gives resultId
		errorReturn to osExit gives 0
		osAllocPages(resultId, _) gives resultId
		osWriteStdout gives 0
		funcAddr(resultId, _) gives resultId
		callIndirect(resultId, _, _, _, _) gives resultId
		// P1.5 subprocess: `osWaitHandle` defines the wait result; the other four define nothing (see above).
		osWaitHandle(resultId, _, _) gives resultId
		osRangePanic to osCloseHandle gives 0
		// `osFreePages` (P1.5-B1a′) defines nothing; it sits past `osWaitHandle` in the union, so its own arm.
		osFreePages gives 0
		// The jump table defines no value.
		switch gives 0
		// A witness call defines its result value, like `callIndirect`. The throwing form reports its PRIMARY,
		// exactly as `tryCall` reports its own and leaves the error flag out of this single-def model.
		witnessCall(resultId, _, _, _, _, _) gives resultId
		witnessTryCall(resultId, _, _, _, _, _, _) gives resultId
		// P1.5 dogfood slice 1a: the value-defining IOCP ops return their captured RAX result id; the rest 0.
		osPipeCreate gives 0
		osIocpCreate(resultId) gives resultId
		osEventCreate(resultId) gives resultId
		osThreadCreate(resultId, _) gives resultId
		osLockInit to osLockLeave gives 0
		osReadOverlapped(resultId, _, _, _, _, _) gives resultId
		osLastError(resultId) gives resultId
		osProcessSpawnInherit gives 0
		osCancelIo gives 0
		osGetPid(resultId) gives resultId
		// P1.5 dogfood slice 1b: osWriteFile returns its captured RAX BOOL id; osPipeCreateOutbound defines nothing.
		osPipeCreateOutbound gives 0
		osWriteFile(resultId, _, _, _, _) gives resultId
		// The wall-clock read defines nothing.
		osReadWallClock gives 0
	end 'r'
end 'stdOpDefinedIdOrZero'

// The base file name (last path segment) of a compiler source path, for the runtime panic message —
// the shv2 equivalent of the bootstrap's `Path.GetFileName`. Strips both separators so a Windows temp
// path and a POSIX one both reduce to `<name>.test`.
function baseName(path String) returns String
	return lastSegment(lastSegment(path, separator: "/"), separator: "\\")
end 'baseName'

function lastSegment(s String, separator String) returns String
	let parts = s.split(separator)
	if parts.isEmpty() 'whole'
		return s
	end 'whole'
	return try parts.get(parts.count() - 1) otherwise s
end 'lastSegment'

// The literal-const map this pass reads is `ConstValueMap` (FoldConstOperands) — each `const` result id
// to the literal it materializes, so a cast/return of a literal is a compile-time decision (E3005 or
// nothing) rather than a runtime guard. It was a byte-identical private copy here (`RangeConstMap`);
// it is now the ONE exported container both Std passes share. Only the POPULATE walk stays this pass's
// own — `collectConsts`, which keeps ONE MAP PER DOMAIN (a float payload is a bit pattern) and
// additionally folds a `neg` of a `const` (`recordConstNeg`) that FoldConstOperands has no reason to.
